    "q-556454": {
      "dom_id": "step-q556454",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following lists $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{23}$ in order from least to greatest?",
      "worked_solution": "To order the irrational numbers $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{23}$ from least to greatest, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each square root lies.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3},$ the closest perfect squares are $1$ and $4.$ Since $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4},$ which means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7},$ the closest perfect squares are $4$ and $9.$ Since $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9},$ which means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{23},$ the closest perfect squares are $16$ and $25.$ Since $16<23<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{23}<\\sqrt{25},$ which means $4<\\sqrt{23}<5.$\n\nComparing these bounds, we can see that the intervals do not overlap and fall into increasing ranges. This gives the order\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{23}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the list in order from least to greatest is $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{23}.$",
