    "q-556455": {
      "dom_id": "step-q556455",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following lists $\\sqrt{29},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ and $\\sqrt{6}$ in order from least to greatest?",
      "worked_solution": "To order the irrational numbers $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ and $\\sqrt{29}$ from least to greatest, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each square root lies.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{6},$ the closest perfect squares are $4$ and $9.$ Since $4<6<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{6}<\\sqrt{9},$ which means $2<\\sqrt{6}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{10},$ the closest perfect squares are $9$ and $16.$ Since $9<10<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{10}<\\sqrt{16},$ which means $3<\\sqrt{10}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{29},$ the closest perfect squares are $25$ and $36.$ Since $25<29<36,$ we have $\\sqrt{25}<\\sqrt{29}<\\sqrt{36},$ which means $5<\\sqrt{29}<6.$\n\nComparing these bounds, we can see that the intervals do not overlap and fall into increasing ranges. This gives the order\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}<\\sqrt{10}<\\sqrt{29}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the list in order from least to greatest is $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{29}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
